# Normain Competitor Benchmark Report

> **Status:** Draft outline. Each section lists what it should contain.

---

## Abstract

- One paragraph, ~150 words
- What was tested (which UCs, which competitors, how many questions in total)
- Who was tested (knowledge-worker AI tools, named)
- Headline numerical result (e.g. "Normain answered correctly on X% of questions vs Y% for the next-best tool")
- Pointer to public data + repo for verification

## Background

- The knowledge-worker AI tool landscape — chat, workspaces, research agents
- Why existing public benchmarks (MMLU, HumanEval, etc.) don't speak to enterprise knowledge work
- The personas this report cares about: decision makers, strategists, operators — not developers
- Brief framing: "what does it mean for an AI tool to be useful at work?" (accuracy, scale, citation discipline, reliability under load)
- Field map of tools considered, with one-line positioning each

## Method

- **Reproduction-level only** — a reader should be able to re-run a UC from this section. No internals, no architecture talk.
- Use-case design: persona, source corpus, question set, expected output format
- For each headline UC: the exact prompt(s) given to each tool, verbatim
- Source materials: what was provided to each tool (file list / URL list), and how (upload / link / workspace ingest)
- Scoring rubric: correct / partial / wrong / retrieval-fail; sources verdict scored separately
- Grading process: LLM-as-judge against ground truth, with human spot-check
- Competitor inclusion criteria: "tools usable by a non-developer knowledge worker today" — explicitly justify exclusions (Claude Code, CLIs, API-only tools)
- Link to public repo + structure.json + ground-truth-eval.json for every UC

## Results

- Chart-first. Minimize prose. Each chart should answer one question.
- **Headline chart:** aggregate correctness across all headline UCs, per tool
- **Per-UC chart:** stacked correct/partial/wrong/retrieval-fail bars per tool, one chart per headline UC
- Optional: time-to-answer chart if it materially supports the story
- Brief caption under each chart — one sentence stating what it shows and the takeaway
- "Field-narrowing" baseline (optional subsection): one chart from a broad-competitor UC showing which tools fell out of contention, justifying the head-to-head focus

## Discussion

- Where Normain leads, and on which workload characteristics
- Where Normain ties or loses, honestly
- Why — workload characteristics (corpus size, question count, dependency depth) that separate the field
- What this implies for buyers choosing between these tools

## Limitations / Scope

- Self-authored benchmark — disclose, don't hide
- UC selection bias — these are the workloads we believe matter; others may not
- Snapshot in time — tool versions and dates tested
- Grading is LLM-judge + human spot-check, not exhaustive human review
- Excluded surfaces (developer tools, API-only) — call out the boundary

## Conclusion

- Two or three sentence TL;DR
- Scope claim: "on these workloads, against these tools, on these dates"
- One-line pointer to the data and the next step for an interested reader
